WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.990
Thank you all for
joining me with me today.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.190
I'll be going through live
demo, as well as some examples

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.060
and background about why R is
very exciting, how you can get

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.350
started in an immediate plan
and then a long-term plan

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.050
about how to be successful using
our [INAUDIBLE] statistical

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:25.550
program.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.450
So first off, a
little bit about me.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.170
Who am I?

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.330
This is my face if you
haven't seen me before

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.130
or met me in person.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:38.080
I originally got
started with statistics

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.060
and using statistical analysis
as a master's student.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.180
I was doing exercise physiology,
looking at how exercise

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.510
is beneficial for brain health.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.490
Now, when I first got
started using statistics,

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.070
I was using mainly point
and click Interfaces, things

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.350
like SPSS, Excel, origin.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.290
So-- [AUDIO OUT] a couple
of different things--

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.680
my statistics in SPSS, my
data cleaning and organization

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.680
in Excel, and then finally,
my plotting and origin.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.800
So it often meant that I had
to change things around or flip

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.250
them when I was switching
between programs.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.680
Eventually, I ended
up in a PHD program,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.470
where I was analyzing
a mouse behavioral data

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.620
with a neurobiology program.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.210
And this is where I really
got my first taste of R.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:27.070
We had an Intro To Statistics
course in my second year.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.485
And we used R for some of
the basics in that course.

00:01:29.485 --> 00:01:31.610
And once I got started with
R, I was like, oh, this

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:32.870
is really exciting.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:35.060
I like the statistical
programming aspect,

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.213
where I was doing coding
and doing everything

00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:38.630
in R, rather than
having to switch

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.580
between a lot of
different programs,

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.410
and got interested in
data science through that.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:47.210
While I had that
course, I did find

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.520
that I needed to
learn a lot more,

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.760
beyond that was just you
given to me in this one month

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.260
or so-- this two
month long course.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.810
So I actually got involved
with the R for DS,

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.720
or R for Data Science Online
Learning Community, which

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.160
is still around today.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.810
And basically,
it's a slack group

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.350
where people are able to go
in, share questions, interact

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.169
with code, or just share ideas.

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:12.350
And out of this was born
the Tidy Tuesday project,

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.330
which is a weekly data project.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.760
And I help post this,
where every week, I

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.380
uploaded data set.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.750
Community members
attack it with R.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.530
And then the Tidyverse, create
plots or statistical analysis

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.582
and then shared the
examples on Twitter.

00:02:26.582 --> 00:02:28.040
And it's been a
really good project

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.902
for continuous learning,
in terms of every week,

00:02:30.902 --> 00:02:31.860
there's a new data set.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.883
So you can track things
out in a different way

00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:37.550
than you're used to, rather than
doing the exact same thing over

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.400
and over, as well as it's
a distributed community.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.890
So even if you don't
have local people who

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.290
are using R around you,
you can learn online

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.840
with an online community.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.080
And lastly, I joined our
studio about a year ago.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.420
I'm on the customer success
team, as Rob mentioned.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.420
And what I do is help our
professional customers better

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.310
use our professional
products, as well as

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.968
open source products to use R
in their enterprise settings.

00:03:02.968 --> 00:03:04.510
What I really want
to get out of this

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.270
is that my journey might
be similar to yours,

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.480
in terms of a lot of
people get started with R

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.670
didn't start as
computer scientist

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:15.490
or didn't start as programming
in other languages.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:19.360
This was their first experience
with using statistics

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.870
and programming together.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.110
And that's kind of
who I'm anticipating

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.490
will be a lot of
the crowd today.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.440
Maybe we'll have some
experienced R users,

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.480
and they can also get
something from this.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:31.700
But I primarily aim this
at people who are just

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.265
getting started with R. So
maybe you've heard of R,

00:03:34.265 --> 00:03:37.130
or you've heard of R studio,
you've heard of the Tidyverse

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.800
But when you first started
trying to get started with R,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.180
youth faced some hurdles.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.000
And so you wanted
to find some more

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.140
examples about how to get
started and be more powerful.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.670
And this really
resonated with me.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.280
And the basis for what
this presentation started

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.310
as was a blog post I wrote a
year ago, based off this tweet

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:59.330
by Jesse, who helped found R for
DS Online Learning Community.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:02.420
And basically, she proposed
that if you'd started R

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:04.370
and didn't end up
continuing with it, what

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.470
were your stumbling blocks?

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:08.900
And some of the common
things that resonated with me

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:10.700
was things like this.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.410
I didn't have examples
of code or people

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.350
doing the basic things
they needed to do,

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.600
basic multivariate tasks.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.640
And I wasn't experienced enough.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.965
For David here, interested in
R, but for a lot of people,

00:04:22.965 --> 00:04:23.840
it was to high level.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.690
So maybe they weren't ready
for full-blown enterprise level

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:27.350
data science.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.850
They were just
trying to get started

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:30.380
with basic statistical
programming

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.780
and using the Tidyverse
to create some plots

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.040
or do some statistical analysis.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.723
And lastly, my contribution was
I know it can be frustrating.

00:04:37.723 --> 00:04:39.140
You're just like,
hey, I just want

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.680
to run my ANOVA in R. Getting
the data in, cleaning it

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.837
up, getting everything set
up can often be a hurdle

00:04:45.837 --> 00:04:46.670
for a lot of people.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:50.680
And I know back in the day,
it was a hurdle for myself.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.250
I'm borrowing this
graph curve here

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.030
from JD Long, who is a data
scientist out in the world--

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:57.858
great guy.

00:04:57.858 --> 00:04:59.400
If you have a chance,
you should look

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.240
at some of his
presentations that he gave

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.680
that our CEO [INAUDIBLE]
2018 or 2019.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.630
And basically, the
premise of this graph

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.790
is that there's
this learning curve.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.330
And as with anything,
when you initially

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.470
get started learning
something new,

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.230
you're here at what they call
the suck threshold, in terms

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.560
of things are hard, and
you're not very powerful.

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.210
With a bad learning
curve, you'll

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.880
actually see that
you stay here in this

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.490
not being able to do
much powerful things

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.940
for a long time.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.480
So the goal for today is to
get us on this good learning

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.730
curve, where you're able to
get into this point of I'm

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:33.350
kicking ass.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:34.890
I'm doing powerful things.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:39.450
I'm creating something useful
in R. And I feel powerful.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:42.360
And hopefully, this will get you
started on this good journey,

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.180
versus the bad journey
[INAUDIBLE] talked

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.070
about previously.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:49.122
So first of all, why learn R?

00:05:49.122 --> 00:05:50.580
I think the biggest
thing for me is

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.710
people will connect with
an amazing community.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.350
Often, when you're doing
stats or programming,

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.420
you might be all by
yourself, in terms

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.550
of you could be siloed
in an academic setting,

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.320
or maybe other people in your
workplace are using just SQL,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.612
or Excel, or some
other language.

00:06:06.612 --> 00:06:08.820
So you're able to connect
with this larger community,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.780
ask them questions, and gain
access to their expertise,

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.300
whether it's through things
like LinkedIn, or Twitter,

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.740
or other different resources,
like Stack Overflow or Studio

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.510
Community.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.180
Additionally, programming
itself is basically a superpower

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.340
that everyone has access to.

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.310
Programming can level up
your skill set over something

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.560
like a graphic user
interface and allow you

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.545
to do things more efficiently.

00:06:33.545 --> 00:06:35.520
And the thing for me
that I really liked

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.710
was that I could do
cleaning, analyzing,

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:40.260
plotting, and finally,
communication around my data

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:41.350
all in one place.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.200
So I didn't have to
switch between a bunch

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:44.830
of different software
suites and paper

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:46.372
a bunch of different
software suites.

00:06:46.372 --> 00:06:48.660
I was able to do
everything in R.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:51.600
Reproducibility is a big thing
with statistical programming,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.740
in terms of your data
product is based off of code.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.113
You have an example of
exactly what you did,

00:06:58.113 --> 00:07:00.030
rather than having to
ask somebody, well, hey,

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:01.760
what did you do to
clean up this data?

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.218
And they're like,
oh, I don't know.

00:07:03.218 --> 00:07:04.050
It was a year ago.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.390
With code, you're able
to actually reference

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.843
exactly what you did
and see what's going on.

00:07:08.843 --> 00:07:10.260
You can do things
like automation,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.270
where if you have
a repeated report,

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.820
you can just rerun your
code with new data.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.980
And it will update
that report for you.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:18.740
And obviously, it's free.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.490
R is and open source language.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:21.660
It's extremely powerful.

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.520
And all the packages that
add additional features to R

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.710
are also free.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:31.080
So it's a great investment
at the very beginning.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.620
We actually ran a survey
right before our last studio

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.950
conference and asked people
what they like best about R.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.120
And again, the community
packages, the Tidyverse,

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:41.880
ggplot.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:45.750
These were some things that
were very popular amongst users.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:48.090
So being able to
be powerful quickly

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.400
and engaging with the
community is something that

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.630
really resonates across groups.

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:56.540
Lastly, hitting again
on why you should

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.880
be excited about
the community is

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:00.673
as Kim says here,
because it's fun,

00:08:00.673 --> 00:08:02.840
and you learn so much without
having to seek it out.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:06.170
So you're just able to passively
take information and learn

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.570
a lot by what other
people are doing.

00:08:08.570 --> 00:08:11.490
[INAUDIBLE] over here has some
other examples, in terms of oh,

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.130
I can actually help
someone out, who's

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.560
six months behind me in
their learning process,

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.070
or commiserate over something
difficult, thank somebody,

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:23.660
see examples of data vis or
other excellent resources.

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:27.380
So hopefully, this reinforces
that the community is great.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:31.310
And you're able to access
them and interact with them.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:34.280
Getting into exactly what
we're covering today,

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:36.409
we'll be looking at the
basics of our studio,

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:39.799
reading data into R,
basics of data wrangling,

00:08:39.799 --> 00:08:43.150
some exploratory data analysis,
basic statistics in R,

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:47.930
and finally, creating
a publication plot.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:51.950
This resonates pretty well
with the total R for DS model

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.070
that Hadley Wickham and
Garrett Grolemund laid out

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.270
in R for data science.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:09:00.560
Their textbook, in terms of
often statistical workflows

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:03.050
can look like this, where
you bring your data into R,

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.360
you tidy or clean it up somehow.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.600
And then, you go through the
cycle of visualizing, modeling,

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:11.690
or statistical testing,
and transformation of data,

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:13.160
eventually creating
a data product

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:16.070
that you communicate, whether
it's R Markdown or Shiny,

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:17.750
or just a simple plot.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.420
And all of this is wrapped
around by programming,

00:09:20.420 --> 00:09:22.820
in terms of rather than having
to point click and do all

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.090
these other things, you're
able to write a flow

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:29.490
and syntax in R. And it will
create these things for you

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:33.420
and able to work through
the workflow here.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:35.420
I really want to emphasize
that the other things

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:37.760
that we're not doing today,
which is covering what

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:39.770
statistical tests to run when.

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.950
We will not be doing a deep-dive
into statistical programming

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:46.520
or data science, in terms of
scaling out to the enterprise.

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:49.005
And I don't expect you to
100% it the first time.

00:09:49.005 --> 00:09:50.630
This is just the
start of your journey.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.777
And I'm hoping to again, get
you on that good learning curve

00:09:53.777 --> 00:09:55.610
and get you started
down a path where you're

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:58.980
able to be successful quickly.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:01.680
And again, that's the ultimate
goal is just eventually,

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.400
you will you'll reach a
point where you're like, hey,

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:04.317
this is really useful.

00:10:04.317 --> 00:10:05.910
I'm actually being
powerful with this.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.430
And you'll feel rewarded
in what you're doing.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.930
As far as R and getting into
the meat of the presentation,

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:15.430
I wanted to emphasize
the difference

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:17.800
between R and RStudio.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:20.890
So if you think of R, it could
be the engine of your vehicle.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:23.290
So it actually will do all
the computation for you.

00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:26.710
It is actually the code that
you're writing and executing,

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.160
whereas RStudio, the IDE
or Integrated Development

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.800
Environment is just an
interface to that engine,

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.710
allows you to have a nice
place to work and write

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:40.000
code, some user enhancements
that I'll walk through.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:43.930
But R itself is
executing everything.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:47.320
This basic example of the
RStudio Cloud interface

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:49.300
shows you the two examples.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.890
So the console would be the
interface directly to R.

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:55.560
So you can write some code here.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:56.860
You execute it.

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:00.170
And this actually created
a plot as its output.

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:05.200
You can also have outputs that
are more numeric or text based.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:06.670
Those can output in the console.

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:09.820
But this right here itself
would be the RStudio interface.

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.490
I'll walk through this, watch
you do a live coding demo.

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:14.890
I just wanted you to get
a very brief overview

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:17.543
of what this looks like.

00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:18.960
The other thing I
wanted to cover,

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:20.760
before we jumped
into live coding

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.660
was it between R and R Packages.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.190
So you can think of
R as a new phone.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.010
It's powerful.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:30.660
It has some core features,
like calling, texting,

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:32.760
email, a web browser.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:36.540
But you need to add additional
applications to do more things.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.510
And R is exactly the same
way, where you can install

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:41.400
packages that add new features.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:45.330
So things like the Tinyverse,
whether it's dplyr or ggplot

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:51.010
allow you to get new features
that you can then use in R.

