00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:05.760
So again, the three, plus three.

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.730
I get my output of six.

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:10.860
The other thing I want
to cover real quickly

00:13:10.860 --> 00:13:12.990
is base R functions and objects.

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.810
So you can do math
or do anything else

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:17.200
and assign it to an object.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.760
So in this case, we'll
assign three, plus five to x.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.710
And then we could
actually call x.

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:25.740
And we can see that it
will then execute the math.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:28.410
Additionally, we can see that
x has been stored as a value

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:29.935
here in our environment.

00:13:29.935 --> 00:13:31.560
So when you're working
with data sets--

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.810
and all important data
set here in a little bit--

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.650
you can actually find them here
