00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:44.333
Always look up here on the top
right for your environment.

00:13:44.333 --> 00:13:45.750
You can also do
some other things,

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:48.430
like combine data
set or numbers.

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:51.477
So here, one through
five is now stored as y.

00:13:51.477 --> 00:13:54.060
And there's some functions that
are just built into R-- things

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:55.477
like sequence,
which actually will

00:13:55.477 --> 00:13:59.520
generate a sequence of
numbers, so zero to 10.

00:13:59.520 --> 00:14:03.090
And it will iterate by
2, so 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10.

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:05.742
These are things that
are all built into R.

00:14:05.742 --> 00:14:07.700
Additionally, you can
write your own functions,

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:10.100
which is where a lot
of power comes from,

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:14.000
in terms of going to create a
function here called add pi.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:16.790
And this function basically
says, take something

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:20.070
that I input, and
add 3.14 to it.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.370
So if I save this
and add pi to it,

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:28.943
we can see that three,
plus 3.14 gets us the 6.14.

00:14:28.943 --> 00:14:30.860
Now, when we're talking
about these functions,

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:33.650
you may not use functions
for quite a while.

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:35.420
But a lot of
libraries are actually

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:36.780
built off of these functions.

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.420
So they give you
more interfaces,

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.600
where rather than having
to type this out itself,

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.310
you just call add pi.

00:14:43.310 --> 00:14:44.960
And similarly, if
we use a library,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:48.050
which is a collection of
functions, we can load it.

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:51.830
And this now gives us a bunch
of plotting functions, based off

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:53.990
of the ggplot library.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:57.100
This will take a data
set-- in this example,

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:58.640
a car is data set--

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:01.050
and allow you to set up a graph.

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.770
So if it actually just
run the code here,

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:04.850
it will input a graph.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.700
And you can see that I
have a graph of horsepower,

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:11.020
versus miles per gallon, and
then a linear model fit to it,

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:13.230
and a title.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.350
The real big thing
that I love about R,

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:17.090
and a lot of the
Tidyverse functions

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:20.540
is that this is very
word based or verb based.

00:15:20.540 --> 00:15:23.010
So I can look at this
and say, OK, well,

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.430
I want my x-axis
to be horsepower.

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:26.690
That's horsepower.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.210
I want my y-axis to
be miles per gallon.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.040
And you can see its
miles per gallon.

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:33.870
And then, I want to add
points to that plot.

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:36.170
So if I just call
this part right here,

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:39.020
we can see that graph of
horsepower vs. Miles per gallon

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.390
and have the nice plots here.

00:15:41.390 --> 00:15:43.520
We can then add
more features to it,

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:46.670
so adding things like the GM
Smooth or that linear model

00:15:46.670 --> 00:15:50.990
fit, and then changing the
title so that they look nicer,

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:54.320
rather than saying HP
and MPG, adding them back

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:58.390
so they say horsepower
and miles per gallon.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:00.220
I'm covering this
pretty quickly.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:04.530
And part of this will be able
to reference it in the future,

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:07.460
but just going through
