So this is quick
exploratory analysis.

00:23:50.340 --> 00:23:52.800
You can see it's very
small amount of code.

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:56.580
You're really just saying what
data set, what are your axes,

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:59.160
and then what kind of
plot do you want to make?

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:01.480
And it will quickly
make this plot for you.

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:04.500
And this shows us that we
have a bimodal distribution.

00:24:04.500 --> 00:24:07.860
We have a group of patients that
have a low Mini-Mental Status

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:10.440
Exam, so lower cognitive
function, compared

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.120
to this patient
population, which

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:14.500
has a higher cognitive
function closer to about 25.

00:24:17.230 --> 00:24:19.480
Now that we've got
an exploratory graph,

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:24.550
maybe you want to take a look
at some basic stats about it.

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:27.070
So we can actually run a
new function, so again,

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:31.200
taking the Rod DF and
then summarizing it.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:33.010
The summarize
function from dplyr

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:36.250
will actually say, OK,
take this data set.

00:24:36.250 --> 00:24:40.040
And then, knock it down
to just these two values.

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:43.020
So I want to look at
the min and the max.

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:46.210
You can see the min here is
8.4, so that's the lowest

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:48.220
Mini-Mental Status Exam value.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:50.600
And the highest is about 28.

00:24:50.600 --> 00:24:54.200
But this just shows you
the raw range across it.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:55.920
That's not all that useful.

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:59.390
So we can actually do
a second function here.

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:01.160
We can use group by.

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:06.860
So rather than just doing it
across the whole data set,

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:09.050
we can actually group
it by health status,

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:12.860
where the health status actually
will show Alzheimer's patients,

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:15.950
versus healthy patients.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:19.310
So we run this, the Rod DF
grouped by health status.

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:20.810
And then, for our
summarize, we want

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:24.860
to get the min value, the
median value, and the max value,

00:25:24.860 --> 00:25:26.930
where these correspond
to other code

00:25:26.930 --> 00:25:31.520
to get started with here
that are part of base R.

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:33.890
So we can see that the
Alzheimer's patients generally

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:36.080
have a lower range.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:38.070
So they have a min of 8.4.

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:39.800
The median is about 15.

00:25:39.800 --> 00:25:41.540
And the max is about 25.

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:43.790
Compared to healthy patients,
we can see all of these

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:48.222
are above 20 for their
Mini-Mental status exam.

00:25:48.222 --> 00:25:49.430
So that's pretty interesting.

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:50.750
But maybe, we want
to see something

00:25:50.750 --> 00:25:51.800
more about the data set.

00:25:51.800 --> 00:25:56.210
So we'll look at Account,
so a table function

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:58.600
of how many of each we have.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:01.120
So again, taking the
Rod DF data frame

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:05.680
and then grouping by both drug
treatment and health status,

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:08.370
where we have a couple of
different doses of the drug.

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:12.100
We use a high dose, a low
dose, and then a placebo.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:13.780
And we give this to
all of our patients

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:16.740
that either have Alzheimer's
or are healthy patients.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:19.760
And we can see that we have
100 of each of these groups.

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:21.580
So each of the groups
have an equal amount

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:23.710
of subjects in them.

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:27.860
So it's a well
set up study here.

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:30.210
When we're thinking more
about some of our statistics,

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:33.340
it might be helpful to get
into some additional graphs.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:36.160
So rather than just
doing a raw distribution,

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:39.820
we might look at it by a
few different groupings.

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:42.610
So again, something more similar
to where the drug treatment,

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:46.020
versus the health
status example.

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:48.180
So in this case, we have ggplot.

00:26:48.180 --> 00:26:51.440
And we're loading our data
set of interest, Rod DF.

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:55.580
We're taking, on the x-axis,
drug treatment, so either

00:26:55.580 --> 00:26:58.240
high-dose, low-dose, or placebo.

00:26:58.240 --> 00:27:01.400
And then, on the y-axis, it
would be a dependent variable.

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:04.220
We'll look at
Mini-Mental Status Exam.

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:08.490
And then, we'll assign
color to be drug treatment.

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:10.210
This will basically
say that there will

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:12.360
be three different colors here.

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:14.330
So each of the drug
treatments will

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:17.490
get to be a different color,
rather than all the same.

00:27:17.490 --> 00:27:21.780
We're going to build a
box plot, so it shows--

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:26.400
sorry about that-- box plot
showing the different examples

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:30.240
of the min, median,
and the range.

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:33.660
And they're going to facet
this or create small multiples,

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:35.115
according to health status.

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:40.330
So if we go here, we
can see for Alzheimer's,

00:27:40.330 --> 00:27:43.420
versus healthy patients,
we can see a nice graph

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:47.770
of the Mini-Mental Status Exam.

00:27:47.770 --> 00:27:51.055
So the high-dose, the
low-dose, and the placebo.

00:27:51.055 --> 00:27:52.680
And then, here in
the healthy patients,

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:55.570
again, the distribution
around that, so the median

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:58.260
and the upper quartiles
for the high-dose, low-dose

00:27:58.260 --> 00:28:00.342
and placebo.

00:28:00.342 --> 00:28:01.800
Something I do want
to point out is

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:05.850
that these axes
aren't super pretty,

00:28:05.850 --> 00:28:08.640
in terms of the drug
treatment has a symbol in it.

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:11.340
Mini-Mental Status
Exam is all lower case.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:14.220
And we'd probably want to flip
this, so placebo, low-dose,

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:16.650
and high-dose are
arranged differently.

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.600
Rather than it going
from high to low,

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:20.790
go from placebo to high.

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:22.650
But we do have a
sense of there appears

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:26.280
to be a difference, in that the
healthy patients, regardless

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:31.338
of treatment, appear to be
having higher MMSE scores,

00:28:31.338 --> 00:28:32.880
compared to the
Alzheimer's patients,

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:35.900
where the placebo
has a low MMSE.

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:37.380
And the low-dose
and the high-dose

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:42.210
increase it slightly with
that treatment there.

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:45.970
Again, just taking a quick
look at the raw data frame,

00:28:45.970 --> 00:28:48.900
we can see re-running
this function,

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:54.060
we have age, sex, health status,
drug treatment, and an MMSE

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:56.690
as our dependent variable.

00:28:56.690 --> 00:28:59.140
It's important to note
that these variables here

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.473
are actually labeled as
just text or character.

00:29:01.473 --> 00:29:02.890
And we actually
want to save those

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:07.840
as a factor, which assigns them
a tier or a categorical label,

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:10.250
as opposed to just raw text.

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:12.460
These are numeric or
doubles, so we can leave them

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:17.140
as is, except for sex
is coded as zero or one,

00:29:17.140 --> 00:29:19.750
where zero is male,
and one is female.

00:29:19.750 --> 00:29:22.300
So we do want to do some data
cleaning here to get everything

00:29:22.300 --> 00:29:25.080
off the ground correctly.

00:29:25.080 --> 00:29:28.730
So we'll actually create a
new data frame or a new object

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:31.350
here, so sum data frame.

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:34.010
We're to take the
original raw data frame,

00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:38.730
and then mutate it, where we're
changing sex to be a factor.

00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:41.157
And the levels are
going to be 0 and 1.

00:29:41.157 --> 00:29:42.740
And then, we're going
to assign labels

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:45.775
to that of male and female.

00:29:45.775 --> 00:29:47.150
So basically, it
will go through.

00:29:47.150 --> 00:29:49.550
R will read and say,
OK, there's zero here.

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:51.350
This will now be male.

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:53.760
There's a one here, so this
will be assigned female.

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.960
And it will change that and
then assign as a factor.

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:59.390
We're also going to change
drug treatment into a factor.

00:29:59.390 --> 00:30:03.080
So we're going to mutate drug
treatment to be a factor,

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:05.060
again as drug treatment,
where the levels are

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:07.495
going to be placebo,
low-dose, and then high-dose.

00:30:07.495 --> 00:30:09.620
So we're actually putting
them in the correct order

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:11.370
we want to see.

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:14.480
Lastly, we're also going to
mutate health status to be

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:17.280
a factor around health status
where the levels are going

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:20.480
to be healthy and
then Alzheimer's.

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:23.130
So if we run this
code, we can see

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:27.790
that the same kind of glimpse
here that we look at the data.

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:29.830
So the variables
all look the same,

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:33.000
but it says male here
as opposed to zero,

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.710
and here's that 1, and so
there should be a female

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:38.610
and we see a female,
and that these are also

00:30:38.610 --> 00:30:39.870
labeled as a factor.

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:42.000
So health status and
drug treatment and sex

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.952
are all labeled as factors now.

00:30:43.952 --> 00:30:45.660
So that's good, because
it gets us set up

00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:51.670
for our future analysis, whether
it's statistics or plotting.

00:30:51.670 --> 00:30:56.270
So again, we're going to
create a summary table here.

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:59.290
So again, this is
all the data set.

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:01.270
So when we're doing
our summary data frame

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:07.810
we want to take a look at means
or medians or standard error

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:09.710
and then the amount of samples.

00:31:09.710 --> 00:31:13.210
So that's what we're doing here
is taking the original data

00:31:13.210 --> 00:31:16.540
frame, reassigning
as the same name.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:17.920
We're going to group this.

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:20.570
So basically take
all of these, group

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:24.220
it by sex, health status,
and drug treatment,

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:27.310
and then summarize around
our functions of interest.

00:31:27.310 --> 00:31:29.020
So we'll get the mean.

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:30.880
We'll get the standard
error as calculated

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:34.492
by standard deviation
by the square root of n.

00:31:34.492 --> 00:31:35.950
And then show the
number of samples

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:39.610
by using the n function
which is for number.

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:42.427
Lastly, we'll also
tell it to a ungroup

00:31:42.427 --> 00:31:44.260
so that it takes away
that grouping variable

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:47.260
after it's already calculated.

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:51.510
So if we run this code, then
we can look at sum df again.

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:54.360
We can see that rather than
having 600 observations,

00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:56.640
we've now gone to
12 observations.

00:31:56.640 --> 00:31:58.220
So basically, the
different groups--

00:31:58.220 --> 00:32:00.990
so male healthy placebo,
male healthy low dose,

00:32:00.990 --> 00:32:03.540
female healthy placebo,
female healthy low dose--

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:05.820
all these have the mean,
the standard error,

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:09.200
the number of samples
calculated out.

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:11.840
So this is helpful in terms
of we've got our nice summary

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:14.270
statistics saved here
and we're ready to work

00:32:14.270 --> 00:32:16.790
with those whether it was
plotting or just saving

00:32:16.790 --> 00:32:19.205
those as a table itself.

00:32:19.205 --> 00:32:21.540
If we wanted to, we could
save it at this point.

00:32:21.540 --> 00:32:25.560
So using our fake drug,
ADX37, the summary

00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:27.800
stats we could
save that as a CSV.

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:29.730
We're just going to keep
working since we know

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:33.360
that we're not stopping here.

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.150
If we did want to create
a summary plot of that,

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:38.170
we can again take
that data frame--

00:32:38.170 --> 00:32:41.640
so the sum df rather than the
raw df that we've been working

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:43.020
with previously--

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:46.990
and again assign
an x and a y axis.

00:32:46.990 --> 00:32:50.520
So here, we're taking the
x-axis, drug treatment,

00:32:50.520 --> 00:32:54.120
the y-axis as mini
mental status exam mean,

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:56.520
we're going to assign a
group, which basically groups

00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:59.670
those numbers together,
drug treatment,

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:01.650
and then a color
of drug treatment.

00:33:01.650 --> 00:33:05.958
So we can see that we get
three colors as opposed to one.

00:33:05.958 --> 00:33:08.000
We're going to increase
the size of the gm point.

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:10.550
So we're going to add
points to our plot

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:12.140
and make them a
little bit bigger.

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:15.002
So if this were say 1,
they'd be nice and tiny.

00:33:15.002 --> 00:33:16.710
But we want it to be
a little bit bigger,

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.580
so we'll do it as 3.

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:23.420
And lastly, we're actually
going to rather than make

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:28.105
one plot where they're
all kind of overlaid here,

00:33:28.105 --> 00:33:29.480
we're going to
facet it or create

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:31.910
small multiples, so facet grid.

00:33:31.910 --> 00:33:37.030
And we're going to make it
where it's sex by health status.

00:33:37.030 --> 00:33:39.640
So if we rerun that code,
we can see that now it's

00:33:39.640 --> 00:33:42.510
separated out into four
plots rather than one,

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:44.250
where male and female
are here, and we

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:46.320
have the healthy and
Alzheimer's patients.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:49.080
And then we finally see the
placebo, low dose, high dose

00:33:49.080 --> 00:33:50.110
for the drug treatment.

00:33:50.110 --> 00:33:52.110
And we can see the kind
of different comparisons

00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:55.300
for the male and female versus
healthy and Alzheimer's.

00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:58.050
So we're getting closer to
a publication plot here,

00:33:58.050 --> 00:34:01.030
but it's still a little rough.

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:02.590
We're finally
getting to the ANOVA

00:34:02.590 --> 00:34:04.600
in terms of walking
through that kind of slowly

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:07.590
and getting set up with the
actual statistical analysis

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:08.721
now.

00:34:08.721 --> 00:34:10.929
So for this example, we're
going to create a new data

00:34:10.929 --> 00:34:14.230
frame called stats data frame
since we already saved this

00:34:14.230 --> 00:34:15.639
as a summary level.

00:34:15.639 --> 00:34:17.830
So actually create
a new data set.

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:20.110
We'll start again with raw df.

00:34:20.110 --> 00:34:22.360
We'll do our mutate again.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:24.190
So we'll change
drug treatment to be

00:34:24.190 --> 00:34:27.070
a factor, sex to be
a factor, and health

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:30.929
status to be a factor so that
it's ready to go for the ANOVA.

00:34:30.929 --> 00:34:32.429
So we'll rerun this.

00:34:32.429 --> 00:34:34.469
And again, we can
see with glimpse

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:37.659
that stats df, our
new data set, is saved

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:39.739
as a factor across the board.

00:34:39.739 --> 00:34:42.310
And this is now labeled as
male and female as opposed

00:34:42.310 --> 00:34:45.690
to 0 and 1.

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:49.520
The actual code for running
ANOVA, at least a type 1

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:53.100
sum of squares, is pretty simple
in terms of the basic setup

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:56.400
is use the AOV or
ANOVA function.

00:34:56.400 --> 00:34:59.280
You assign the dependent
variable first,

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:02.188
use a tilde and then the
independent variable,

00:35:02.188 --> 00:35:04.230
and you tell it what is
the data set of interest.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:08.680
So here, we're using a
fake data set, data df.

00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:11.650
If you want to do main
effects or interactions

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:13.540
there is a slight
variation there.

00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:16.090
So for just main
effects you will

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:20.200
add your dependent variable,
your independent label 1.

00:35:20.200 --> 00:35:22.990
So here sex, drug
treatment, health status

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:25.910
are all added individually
as main effects.

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:27.910
Or you can do main
effects interactions

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:32.340
by using a star
instead of a plus sign.

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:34.970
There is a couple of different
ways of doing ANOVAs.

00:35:34.970 --> 00:35:36.520
I'm just showing
one example here,

00:35:36.520 --> 00:35:39.020
and there's a whole lot of other
different statistical tests

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:42.770
that you can do in R. So again,
this is just a basic example

00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:44.600
and there's some
textbooks I'll be sharing

00:35:44.600 --> 00:35:47.670
about great examples of
going through choosing

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:49.910
a different statistical
test or approaching it

00:35:49.910 --> 00:35:52.460
from a statistical
validity standpoint.

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:56.770
Just trying to get us started
with the basic setup here.

00:35:56.770 --> 00:36:00.490
I did want to point out that you
can use the plus or the star,

00:36:00.490 --> 00:36:02.320
but we don't want
to use commas here.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:04.567
This would actually throw
an error, and that's OK.

00:36:04.567 --> 00:36:05.650
It doesn't break anything.

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:07.750
It just says, you know,
it's not able to do this

00:36:07.750 --> 00:36:11.170
because it's trying to assign
this in an improper way.

00:36:11.170 --> 00:36:14.110
So always make sure and use a
plus to add only main effects,

00:36:14.110 --> 00:36:17.980
or the star for the main
effects and interactions.

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:20.340
So we'll run it as the main
effects and interactions.

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:23.650
And now we can see that it's
saved our Alzheimer's Disease

00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:25.860
ANOVA as a list object here.

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:27.562
And there's a bunch
of things going on.

00:36:27.562 --> 00:36:29.520
And I will show what all
those different things

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:30.780
are here in one second.

00:36:33.400 --> 00:36:38.640
So if we actually call
summary on that object, ADAOV,

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:40.530
you kind of get
a nice table here

00:36:40.530 --> 00:36:42.200
of what you would expect to see.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:45.900
Your degrees of freedom, sum
of squares, mean square, your F

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:47.415
value, and then your p value.

00:36:47.415 --> 00:36:50.590
And it also tells you
the significance levels,

00:36:50.590 --> 00:36:53.460
whether it's 0.1 or
all the way down to 0.

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.610
So you can see that there are
significant main effects as

00:36:56.610 --> 00:36:59.590
well as an interaction of drug
treatment by health status.

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:03.360
So we'll follow up with
those, but this kind of output

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:05.970
here is not always
the most useful

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:08.950
in terms of working
with it in other ways.

00:37:08.950 --> 00:37:13.240
So we can actually tidy this
up with the broom package.

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:17.372
And if we were to
look at tidy ad ANOVA,

00:37:17.372 --> 00:37:19.330
we can see that it's in
a different format now.

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:20.810
It's a little bit cleaner.

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:25.030
So rather than having
this kind of ASIC output,

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:27.432
we've got it in a nice
data frame where you're

00:37:27.432 --> 00:37:29.140
able to see all the
different things kind

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:32.170
of across a little bit cleaner.

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:35.110
And again, you could save
this to Excel or as a CSV

00:37:35.110 --> 00:37:37.300
if you wanted to export
it, or you can just kind of

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:38.920
keep it here.

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:41.500
Because we did see main
effects and interactions,

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:43.810
we should follow up
with post hoc tests.

00:37:43.810 --> 00:37:46.330
So we'll take a look at a couple
of different post hoc test

00:37:46.330 --> 00:37:47.750
examples.

00:37:47.750 --> 00:37:50.230
The first one would just
be a pairwise t-test

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:53.070
with the Bonferroni correction.

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:57.780
And the basic setup
here, if we take a look,

00:37:57.780 --> 00:38:00.180
is that we're going to
save it as an AD Pairwise

00:38:00.180 --> 00:38:02.250
so we know which one this was.

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:05.450
The pairwise t-test
function gets us started.

00:38:05.450 --> 00:38:09.540
And we're going to assign
a stats df with MMSE

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.920
as our data set
of interest where

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:15.100
MMSE is the dependent variable.

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:17.620
And then we've got a little
bit weirder syntax here

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:20.300
that's hard to read but
I'll walk through with it.

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:22.950
So again, we're using
the stats df data frame,

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:26.950
and we're using the sex
column as the factor.

00:38:26.950 --> 00:38:32.110
We're doing an interaction,
shown here with the colon.

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:34.780
And then using the
next factor, which

00:38:34.780 --> 00:38:38.590
is drug treatment, another
colon for the interaction,

00:38:38.590 --> 00:38:42.610
and then the stats df and
the health status column.

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:45.670
And lastly, telling it to
do a p-value adjustment

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:48.790
for multiple comparisons
of Bonferroni.

00:38:48.790 --> 00:38:50.470
So we run that, and
we can again see

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:53.590
that it's saved as an object.

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:55.860
Again, I like to clean
this up a little bit

00:38:55.860 --> 00:38:57.820
so I'll run tidy on it.

00:38:57.820 --> 00:39:00.640
So we use the broom
package to tidy it.

00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:03.030
And I'm actually going
to then mutate the p

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:08.520
value to round it so it's less
expansive rather than sig figs.

00:39:08.520 --> 00:39:12.055
We kind of get this nice
just five significant units.

00:39:12.055 --> 00:39:13.430
So we can see the
comparison here

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:17.210
where we're saying comparison
one, male placebo Alzheimer's

00:39:17.210 --> 00:39:19.850
compared to male placebo
healthy is significantly

00:39:19.850 --> 00:39:23.870
different as opposed to the
low dose in the healthy male

00:39:23.870 --> 00:39:26.270
versus the placebo in
the healthy male not

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:28.850
statistically
significantly different.

00:39:28.850 --> 00:39:32.440
That's just the quick
example of the Bonferroni.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:36.060
If maybe you like the Tukey test
better you could also do that.

00:39:36.060 --> 00:39:39.110
So here, we're using
the Tukey HSD function.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:43.370
We're using the AD ANOVA data
set we've already created.

00:39:43.370 --> 00:39:46.215
So that was our actual
ANOVA output from above.

00:39:46.215 --> 00:39:47.840
And this syntax is
a little bit cleaner

00:39:47.840 --> 00:39:51.470
where we say, run a
Tukey on this ANOVA

00:39:51.470 --> 00:39:54.920
where the interaction of sex,
drug treatment, and health

00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:56.780
status is of interest.

00:39:56.780 --> 00:40:00.110
We're then going to tidy it
to clean it up like this,

00:40:00.110 --> 00:40:03.170
take just the first
six values with head,

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:04.776
and create a nice table.

00:40:04.776 --> 00:40:07.540
So if I do this,
we can again see

00:40:07.540 --> 00:40:10.040
that we have our term,
the comparison here.

00:40:10.040 --> 00:40:13.750
So female placebo healthy
versus male placebo healthy.

00:40:13.750 --> 00:40:17.230
It's got your confidence
intervals low and high,

00:40:17.230 --> 00:40:20.550
and then the adjusted p value
here for the Tukey test.

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:22.800
These are going to be pretty
similar to the Bonferroni

00:40:22.800 --> 00:40:24.760
because the comparisons are--

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:27.570
or sorry, the groups are
very, very different,

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:29.220
but you'll obviously
need to choose

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:33.918
whichever statistical test
makes sense for your analysis.

00:40:33.918 --> 00:40:35.460
Lastly, in the next
couple of minutes

00:40:35.460 --> 00:40:39.120
I'm going to go through a quick
guide to the publication graph.

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:43.380
So I'm actually going to create
a data set and actually can

00:40:43.380 --> 00:40:47.220
create this with the
tribble function, where

00:40:47.220 --> 00:40:50.910
I can assign a column and then
all the different values in it.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:54.030
So if I run this
function here, you

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:57.030
can see that column
A gets A, B and C--

00:40:57.030 --> 00:41:02.310
A, B and C. And column
B gets 1, 2, 3--

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:04.570
1, 2, 3 in it.

00:41:04.570 --> 00:41:06.820
We assign the column names
by adding a tilde before it

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:11.440
and then each of the columns
below it get added correctly.

00:41:11.440 --> 00:41:13.570
Rather than just
adding random data,

00:41:13.570 --> 00:41:16.270
I'm going to assign these
as the proper groups.

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:17.710
So I actually
looked back through

00:41:17.710 --> 00:41:20.260
the pairwise comparisons above
and found the groups that

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:22.040
were significantly different.

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:24.580
So the low dose Alzheimer's
male and the high dose

00:41:24.580 --> 00:41:27.428
Alzheimer's male
versus the placebo.

00:41:27.428 --> 00:41:29.095
And then the low dose
Alzheimer's female

00:41:29.095 --> 00:41:31.430
and the high dose Alzheimer's
female versus placebo

00:41:31.430 --> 00:41:33.140
was significantly different.

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:36.800
So we're creating a new
data set based around that.

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:39.200
And then reassigning our
factor levels there so

00:41:39.200 --> 00:41:43.040
we see drug treatment, sex as
a factor, and health status

00:41:43.040 --> 00:41:45.300
as a factor as well.

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.760
We're finally getting to
that publication plot,

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:51.720
and it looks a lot wordier
or longer than our plots

00:41:51.720 --> 00:41:52.380
from above.

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:57.300
That's because we're doing
some more customization.

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:59.307
So if we look at the
actual plot here--

00:41:59.307 --> 00:42:01.640
and I'll pull it up in the
window so you can see it nice

00:42:01.640 --> 00:42:02.200
and big--

00:42:04.740 --> 00:42:07.120
there you go.

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:10.010
You can see a few
different things.

00:42:10.010 --> 00:42:13.570
So we're doing our basic
setup where we have our data

00:42:13.570 --> 00:42:15.220
as the sum data frame.

00:42:15.220 --> 00:42:17.680
We're assigning X as
the drug treatment and Y

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:21.370
as the mini mental
status exam mean.

00:42:21.370 --> 00:42:23.560
We're going to fill it or
assign a color according

00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:27.130
to drug treatment and
group it by drug treatment.

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:29.030
And then we're going
to add error bars.

00:42:29.030 --> 00:42:32.488
So this white, gray, black
is according to the fill,

00:42:32.488 --> 00:42:34.780
and then we've assigned the
error bars which are really

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:37.840
small here according to group.

00:42:37.840 --> 00:42:41.770
I built out that
data set above here

00:42:41.770 --> 00:42:47.210
because I'm actually assigning
small asterisks to the graph.

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:51.440
And that can be found with the
gm text I've assigned here.

00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:54.110
So this is data according
to the sig fig data frame

00:42:54.110 --> 00:42:58.520
that we set up and adding
a label of an asterisk.

00:42:58.520 --> 00:43:01.640
I also changed a couple
of the theme items,

00:43:01.640 --> 00:43:05.360
so changing it to be a white
background versus a gray,

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:07.700
changing the access text size.

00:43:07.700 --> 00:43:10.940
All these different things are
kind of at your discretion.

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:13.670
So maybe you're like oh I want
to add these different changes

00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:15.440
or make it a little
bit prettier,

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:17.505
this would be an example.

00:43:17.505 --> 00:43:19.880
I'm going to leave this here
for you to play around with.

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:22.560
So I will be sharing
that at cloud setup

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:24.320
so you can actually
take a look at this

00:43:24.320 --> 00:43:27.080
and pull out different
things and try it out.

00:43:27.080 --> 00:43:30.170
But the basic idea is
that we create based off

00:43:30.170 --> 00:43:31.250
of the data set.

00:43:31.250 --> 00:43:35.660
We assign the x and y axes.

00:43:35.660 --> 00:43:38.810
What type of graph do we want
to make-- an error bar, a bar

00:43:38.810 --> 00:43:39.850
graph.

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:42.320
And then we want to make small
multiples or multiple graphs

00:43:42.320 --> 00:43:44.150
according to sex
and health status

00:43:44.150 --> 00:43:47.740
where we see sex and
health status again.

00:43:47.740 --> 00:43:49.370
Lastly, we'll add our labels.

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:53.462
So rather than kind of
blah or small descriptors

00:43:53.462 --> 00:43:55.420
we have a nice big drug
treatment and cognitive

00:43:55.420 --> 00:43:57.850
function, and then a
figure caption here.

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:02.560
So Figure 1, the effect of novel
drug treatment with our groups

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:07.440
and the total, as well as
a significant indicator.

00:44:07.440 --> 00:44:11.580
Lastly, we can take that graph
that we saved as g1, give it

00:44:11.580 --> 00:44:12.330
a nice title.

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:14.880
So [INAUDIBLE]
publication graph.

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:19.830
Assign a height, a width,
units for that, and then a DPI.

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:21.940
The DPI indicates
the dots per inch,

00:44:21.940 --> 00:44:26.100
which basically says a high
quality as that increases.

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:28.350
If you don't do that, it
might be a little bit fuzzy.

00:44:28.350 --> 00:44:30.570
As you can see, this
is a little bit fuzzy.

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:32.520
Versus if we look
at the publication

00:44:32.520 --> 00:44:36.060
graph here, very nice and sharp
and it's actually very large

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:38.190
and you can zoom in
and it looks good.

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:44.010
I'll leave this part out.

00:44:44.010 --> 00:44:46.580
And I think we're good there.

00:44:46.580 --> 00:44:50.780
I really quickly want to go
back into our presentation

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:52.730
now that we've covered this.

00:44:52.730 --> 00:44:55.010
And we'll walk through
the last few stages

00:44:55.010 --> 00:44:57.170
on what to do next now
that you've kind of gotten

00:44:57.170 --> 00:45:01.430
a whirlwind walk through
of how to do statistics

00:45:01.430 --> 00:45:05.612
and some live coding in
R. So for the next steps,

00:45:05.612 --> 00:45:07.070
and the kind of
the follow up items

00:45:07.070 --> 00:45:09.260
that might be helpful for you.

00:45:09.260 --> 00:45:11.820
I will be sharing the slides
in RStudio Colab Instance,

00:45:11.820 --> 00:45:14.490
so you'll have the
availability of all this code

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:17.962
and be able to play around with
this data set in RStudio Cloud

00:45:17.962 --> 00:45:19.420
without having to
install anything.

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:21.320
You just do it through
your web browser.

00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:23.820
So you'll be able to kind of
walk through exactly what I did

00:45:23.820 --> 00:45:26.240
and play around with it.

00:45:26.240 --> 00:45:28.940
Additionally, working
inside RStudio Cloud

00:45:28.940 --> 00:45:32.670
there's actually training
data sets and examples there.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:35.780
So if you go to RStudio Cloud
Primers it will actually

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:38.540
show you the basics of
how to get started with R,

00:45:38.540 --> 00:45:42.590
how to work with data, do
some visualization, tidying,

00:45:42.590 --> 00:45:46.250
iteration, which is more of high
level functional programming,

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:47.960
and then writing
functions themselves

00:45:47.960 --> 00:45:49.927
which I covered very quickly.

00:45:49.927 --> 00:45:52.010
The good thing here is
that these are interactive,

00:45:52.010 --> 00:45:55.060
so you're able to write code
and get feedback immediately.

00:45:55.060 --> 00:45:57.310
They're ready to go without
having to install anything

00:45:57.310 --> 00:45:58.597
and you can't break anything.

00:45:58.597 --> 00:46:00.680
You don't have to worry
about installing something

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:03.080
on a Windows versus a
Mac versus a Linux box,

00:46:03.080 --> 00:46:06.620
it's just ready to go
in your web browser.

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:08.330
There is additionally
some great books

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:09.470
if you're trying
to figure out, OK

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:11.270
which statistical
test do I need to use.

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:14.050
Learning Statistics with
R by Danielle Navarro

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:17.900
is a great book that covers the
didactic understanding of why

00:46:17.900 --> 00:46:21.170
statistics in which to
use, as well as raw R code

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:22.820
about how to do them.

00:46:22.820 --> 00:46:24.800
The R for Data
Science book actually

00:46:24.800 --> 00:46:29.120
covers examples of going
through R for data science

00:46:29.120 --> 00:46:32.000
as opposed to pure statistics,
so doing more programming,

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:34.760
plotting, exploratory
data analysis.

00:46:34.760 --> 00:46:37.040
This is a great textbook by
Hadley Wickham and Garret

00:46:37.040 --> 00:46:37.762
Grolemund.

00:46:37.762 --> 00:46:39.470
And if you're more
interested just purely

00:46:39.470 --> 00:46:43.250
in data visualization,
Social Viz by Kieran Healy

00:46:43.250 --> 00:46:46.820
is a great resource for
seeing example R code

00:46:46.820 --> 00:46:49.220
to create these beautiful
plots and examples like you

00:46:49.220 --> 00:46:51.700
saw today.

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:54.100
The last thing I wanted
to go over as well

00:46:54.100 --> 00:46:57.520
was the Tidy Tuesday R4DS
Online Learning Community.

00:46:57.520 --> 00:47:01.250
So this is that weekly project
I talked about briefly.

00:47:01.250 --> 00:47:05.020
Basically a data set gets
uploaded weekly at GitHub.

00:47:05.020 --> 00:47:08.440
So GitHub R4DS Tidy Tuesday.

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:11.260
Every Monday on Twitter
we release the data set

00:47:11.260 --> 00:47:12.320
and it looks like this.

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:15.460
So a link to the data, an
article about the data,

00:47:15.460 --> 00:47:17.755
and then some example plots.

00:47:17.755 --> 00:47:19.630
Basically, you just play
around with the data

00:47:19.630 --> 00:47:21.305
and you can share
it or just observe

00:47:21.305 --> 00:47:22.930
what other people
are doing on Twitter.

00:47:22.930 --> 00:47:24.820
And it's a great
way to learn more

00:47:24.820 --> 00:47:30.335
about using R in a Tidyverse
with new data sets each week.

00:47:30.335 --> 00:47:31.960
Additionally, there's
RStudio Community

00:47:31.960 --> 00:47:33.650
where you can ask questions.

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:35.770
So whether it's
questions about IDE

00:47:35.770 --> 00:47:38.290
or if you need help with
actual code you can go in here

00:47:38.290 --> 00:47:40.760
and ask questions there.

00:47:40.760 --> 00:47:43.320
The R4DS online
learning community

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:45.660
has a slack group where
you can also ask questions

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:47.452
and it's a little bit
more private since it

00:47:47.452 --> 00:47:49.410
isn't a public facing group.

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:52.203
Anyone can join, but it isn't--

00:47:52.203 --> 00:47:54.120
you have to log in to
slack at the very least,

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:57.000
so it sometimes
feels a little bit

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.590
quicker to start as you're able
to do immediate interaction.

00:48:00.590 --> 00:48:03.510
And lastly, R is pretty
active on Twitter.

00:48:03.510 --> 00:48:06.910
Somebody actually collected all
the tweets using the R Stats

00:48:06.910 --> 00:48:10.290
hashtag between 2008 and 2018.

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:12.480
You can see there's
almost 500,000 or about

00:48:12.480 --> 00:48:15.030
430,000 tweets.

00:48:15.030 --> 00:48:17.430
And there are
additional hashtags

00:48:17.430 --> 00:48:21.060
you can follow whether it's
RStats or Tidyverse, ggplot,

00:48:21.060 --> 00:48:23.640
epidemiology,
Twitter, R Spatial,

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:26.190
there's a bunch of
different resources there.

00:48:28.740 --> 00:48:30.840
R Open Sci has a
lot of packages that

00:48:30.840 --> 00:48:33.420
may be more specific to
your type of science.

00:48:33.420 --> 00:48:36.600
So I covered one example
for kind of neuroscience.

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:41.007
Maybe you're doing plant science
or marine biology or something,

00:48:41.007 --> 00:48:42.840
there could be other
packages through R Open

00:48:42.840 --> 00:48:43.673
Sci that are useful.

00:48:43.673 --> 00:48:47.120
And that's just ropensci.com.

00:48:47.120 --> 00:48:49.620
And lastly, if you're
trying to get involved,

00:48:49.620 --> 00:48:52.130
the R Ladies global
community is great.

00:48:52.130 --> 00:48:55.430
And basically their mission
is to promote gender diversity

00:48:55.430 --> 00:48:56.660
in R community.

00:48:56.660 --> 00:48:59.180
So this includes under
representation of any minority

00:48:59.180 --> 00:48:59.970
gender.

00:48:59.970 --> 00:49:02.270
You're able to join
remotely or at actual

00:49:02.270 --> 00:49:05.600
meet ups in your city, and
it's a great initiative

00:49:05.600 --> 00:49:10.340
to staying involved and
contributing to this community.

00:49:10.340 --> 00:49:12.800
And lastly, I know that
this kind of was very quick

00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:16.240
and it can seem intimidating
to get started with R.

00:49:16.240 --> 00:49:19.160
And maybe you're like oh,
I'm later in my career

00:49:19.160 --> 00:49:21.170
or I'm a little bit older.

00:49:21.170 --> 00:49:22.910
Jenny Bryan, who I
have a huge amount

00:49:22.910 --> 00:49:27.080
of appreciation and respect for,
talking about this with Sharla

00:49:27.080 --> 00:49:30.680
and talking about how everyone
has their own career path.

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:33.090
And it can be a slow career
movement or a fast curve

00:49:33.090 --> 00:49:33.590
movement.

00:49:33.590 --> 00:49:35.360
That's OK.

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:37.695
So it kind of
reinforcers that you

00:49:37.695 --> 00:49:39.320
don't have to get
started super quickly

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:43.130
and you can kind of go
through it at your pace.

00:49:43.130 --> 00:49:45.710
And then lastly,
obviously Hadly Wickam

00:49:45.710 --> 00:49:47.720
is pretty well known
in the R community

00:49:47.720 --> 00:49:50.240
and he's still Googles
stuff and finds it

00:49:50.240 --> 00:49:51.770
through the Rstats hashtag.

00:49:51.770 --> 00:49:54.200
So again, no matter how
experienced you are,

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:56.900
it's OK to make mistakes,
it's OK to be learning,

00:49:56.900 --> 00:50:00.660
and we're all here
to learn with you so.

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:03.450
With that, I'll actually
end a little bit after time

00:50:03.450 --> 00:50:05.320
but still have some
time for questions.

00:50:05.320 --> 00:50:08.220
I appreciate your
attendance today

00:50:08.220 --> 00:50:09.610
and I'll take questions now.

00:50:09.610 --> 00:50:12.280
Thanks.

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:15.160
So the key differences between
RStudio Cloud and RStudio

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:17.950
is RStudio Cloud is hosted,
so you connect to it

00:50:17.950 --> 00:50:19.180
purely through a web browser.

00:50:19.180 --> 00:50:21.100
You don't have to
install anything.

00:50:21.100 --> 00:50:24.550
And then the RStudio Desktop
is actually installed

00:50:24.550 --> 00:50:26.620
on your computer,
whether it's your laptop

00:50:26.620 --> 00:50:28.570
or your desktop computer.

00:50:28.570 --> 00:50:30.910
Both will look exactly the
same once you're actually

00:50:30.910 --> 00:50:33.700
using them.

00:50:33.700 --> 00:50:35.860
OK, some people asked about
the Tidyverse library.

00:50:35.860 --> 00:50:42.150
It's basically a [AUDIO OUT]
so packages give you

00:50:42.150 --> 00:50:45.060
those functions
that allow you to do

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:47.620
more things in R without
having to write it by hand.

00:50:47.620 --> 00:50:49.500
And the Tidyverse
a meta-collection

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:52.635
of other packages that
fall along in that syntax.

00:50:57.140 --> 00:50:59.330
You can actually create
websites using RMarkdown.

00:50:59.330 --> 00:51:01.340
It produces HTML content.

00:51:01.340 --> 00:51:06.800
So you can look at the blogdown
or the distill packages.

00:51:06.800 --> 00:51:09.890
Those actually allow you to
create entire websites or blogs

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:13.010
in R. And that's how I
run my personal blog.

00:51:13.010 --> 00:51:15.445
There's some great
resources at blogdown.

00:51:15.445 --> 00:51:16.820
There's actually
a book about it.

00:51:16.820 --> 00:51:18.420
And distill has
its own website--

00:51:18.420 --> 00:51:21.950
distill.rstudio.com.

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:22.730
There we go.

00:51:22.730 --> 00:51:25.370
So this actually walks
through kind of in long form

00:51:25.370 --> 00:51:27.800
exactly what we covered today.

00:51:27.800 --> 00:51:30.200
I didn't want to just read
this out in front of you,

00:51:30.200 --> 00:51:32.750
but it basically gives a similar
presentation to what I did.

00:51:32.750 --> 00:51:35.370
Although I have changed it
up a little bit since then.

00:51:35.370 --> 00:51:38.150
But this could be useful if you
maybe don't like videos as much

00:51:38.150 --> 00:51:40.040
and you want to read
through something.

00:51:40.040 --> 00:51:42.800
You can just search for
the mockup.netlify.com.

00:51:42.800 --> 00:51:46.380
It's actually the first
post I have there.

00:51:46.380 --> 00:51:48.560
The Tidyverse is
one package but it's

00:51:48.560 --> 00:51:49.955
a meta collection of packages.

00:51:53.370 --> 00:51:55.488
You can actually--
someone's asking

00:51:55.488 --> 00:51:57.030
about how do you
know which functions

00:51:57.030 --> 00:51:58.500
are available in each package.

00:51:58.500 --> 00:52:02.220
A lot of packages will have
their own website or a CRAN

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:02.800
page.

00:52:02.800 --> 00:52:10.508
So if you think of like
the Tidyverse, [INAUDIBLE]

00:52:10.508 --> 00:52:12.800
website where you can look
at all the different things.

00:52:12.800 --> 00:52:14.997
So it's just tidyverse.org.

00:52:14.997 --> 00:52:16.580
You can look at the
different packages

00:52:16.580 --> 00:52:18.290
that are here and available.

00:52:18.290 --> 00:52:20.550
You can click on the
individual packages

00:52:20.550 --> 00:52:24.620
and there's everything from
cheat sheets to example usage,

00:52:24.620 --> 00:52:29.180
where it is in the lifecycle,
and then references to all

00:52:29.180 --> 00:52:32.470
of the different functions
that are available in it.

00:52:32.470 --> 00:52:37.060
You can also do this from
within RStudio Cloud.

00:52:37.060 --> 00:52:41.490
So if you-- let's see if
we can get back in there--

00:52:41.490 --> 00:52:45.120
I'll log back in real quickly.

00:52:45.120 --> 00:52:47.790
So you can actually
just type some code.

00:52:47.790 --> 00:52:51.300
It will kind of show you
exactly what that function is.

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:58.080
So if you did like question
dpyr or question mark ggplot2,

00:52:58.080 --> 00:53:02.130
that will open up in the Help
pain an example about that.

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:06.240
So it will give you links
to external resources

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:08.910
as well as some information
about the package.

00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:11.160
You can also do that
same syntax in terms

00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:15.480
of question mark followed
by the package name

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:18.300
and then tell it what are the
functions you're looking at.

00:53:18.300 --> 00:53:22.470
So let's do annotate.

00:53:22.470 --> 00:53:24.090
So if I did it on
just one function

00:53:24.090 --> 00:53:27.810
it will give a description,
the example arguments

00:53:27.810 --> 00:53:33.120
that are available, the details
and a quick example of it.

00:53:33.120 --> 00:53:35.160
You can label a
factor as anything.

00:53:35.160 --> 00:53:37.800
So someone's asking about
labeling a factor as say you

00:53:37.800 --> 00:53:41.090
like less than 24 as
opposed to 24 written out

00:53:41.090 --> 00:53:44.490
as text, that label could
be used as a character.

00:53:44.490 --> 00:53:47.820
So you could actually
just type in less than 24.

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:56.733
So example like factor
x label less than 24.

00:53:56.733 --> 00:53:57.900
Something along those lines.

00:53:57.900 --> 00:53:59.817
This wouldn't actually
give you the full code,

00:53:59.817 --> 00:54:02.810
but just an example of this
is now a character vector

00:54:02.810 --> 00:54:05.050
and could be labeled that way.

